# Introduction

As applications grow, developers often create one large interface that contains many unrelated methods.

Initially, this may seem convenient, but over time it creates problems:

- Classes implement methods they don't need.
- Developers write empty methods.
- Developers throw `NotSupportedException`.
- Code becomes difficult to maintain.

ISP solves this problem.

---

# Definition

The Interface Segregation Principle states:

> **Clients should not be forced to depend upon interfaces they do not use.**

— Robert C. Martin

---

# Simple Meaning

Instead of creating one huge interface,

Create several small interfaces.

Each interface should represent **one capability**.

---

# Why Do We Need ISP?

Imagine your company develops an Employee Management System.

Someone creates this interface.

```csharp
public interface IEmployeeService
{
    void AddEmployee(Employee employee);

    void UpdateEmployee(Employee employee);

    void DeleteEmployee(int id);

    Employee GetEmployee(int id);

    void GenerateSalary();

    void SendEmail();

    void ExportToExcel();

    void PrintReport();
}
```

At first glance, everything looks fine.

But different modules use different features.

---

# HR Module

Needs only

```
AddEmployee()

UpdateEmployee()

DeleteEmployee()

GetEmployee()
```

---

# Finance Module

Needs only

```
GenerateSalary()
```

---

# Reporting Module

Needs only

```
ExportToExcel()

PrintReport()
```

---

# Notification Module

Needs only

```
SendEmail()
```

---

# What is a Fat Interface?

An interface containing unrelated methods is called a **Fat Interface**.

Example

```
IEmployeeService

├── Employee Management

├── Salary

├── Email

├── Reports
```

Everything is combined into one interface.

This violates ISP.

---

# Problem with Fat Interfaces

Suppose Finance creates this class.

```csharp
public class SalaryService : IEmployeeService
{
    public void GenerateSalary()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Salary Generated");
    }

    public void AddEmployee(Employee employee)
    {
        throw new NotSupportedException();
    }

    public void UpdateEmployee(Employee employee)
    {
        throw new NotSupportedException();
    }

    public void DeleteEmployee(int id)
    {
        throw new NotSupportedException();
    }

    public Employee GetEmployee(int id)
    {
        throw new NotSupportedException();
    }

    public void SendEmail()
    {
        throw new NotSupportedException();
    }

    public void ExportToExcel()
    {
        throw new NotSupportedException();
    }

    public void PrintReport()
    {
        throw new NotSupportedException();
    }
}
```

---

# Why Is This Bad?

Notice these methods:

```csharp
throw new NotSupportedException();
```

The class is being forced to implement functionality that it does not support.

This is an ISP violation.

---

# Solution – Split the Interface

Instead of one large interface,

Create multiple small interfaces.

---

## Employee Management

```csharp
public interface IEmployeeManagement
{
    void AddEmployee(Employee employee);

    void UpdateEmployee(Employee employee);

    void DeleteEmployee(int id);

    Employee GetEmployee(int id);
}
```

---

## Salary

```csharp
public interface ISalaryService
{
    void GenerateSalary();
}
```

---

## Email

```csharp
public interface IEmailService
{
    void SendEmail();
}
```

---

## Reports

```csharp
public interface IReportService
{
    void ExportToExcel();

    void PrintReport();
}
```

Now each class implements only the interface it needs.

