# Payment System Example

Imagine this interface.

```csharp
public interface IPayment
{
    void Pay(decimal amount);

    void Refund(decimal amount);

    void CancelPayment();

    void GenerateInvoice();
}
```

Credit Card supports everything.

Gift Card supports only payment.

A developer writes

```csharp
public class GiftCardPayment : IPayment
{
    public void Pay(decimal amount)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Gift Card Payment");
    }

    public void Refund(decimal amount)
    {
        throw new NotSupportedException();
    }

    public void CancelPayment()
    {
        throw new NotSupportedException();
    }

    public void GenerateInvoice()
    {
        throw new NotSupportedException();
    }
}
```

Again,

```
NotSupportedException
```

The interface is too large.

---

# Better Payment Design

Split it.

```csharp
public interface IPayment
{
    void Pay(decimal amount);
}
```

```csharp
public interface IRefundable
{
    void Refund(decimal amount);
}
```

```csharp
public interface ICancelable
{
    void CancelPayment();
}
```

```csharp
public interface IInvoiceGenerator
{
    void GenerateInvoice();
}
```

Now

```csharp
public class CreditCardPayment :
    IPayment,
    IRefundable,
    ICancelable,
    IInvoiceGenerator
{
}
```

Gift Card

```csharp
public class GiftCardPayment : IPayment
{
    public void Pay(decimal amount)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Gift Card Payment");
    }
}
```

No unnecessary methods.

ISP is satisfied.

