# Summary

The Interface Segregation Principle encourages designing **small, focused interfaces** instead of large, all-purpose interfaces.

Each class should implement only the capabilities it actually supports.

This leads to cleaner code, better maintainability, easier testing, and fewer runtime issues like `NotSupportedException`.

---

# Key Takeaways

✔ Clients should not depend on methods they don't use.

✔ Avoid Fat Interfaces.

✔ Split interfaces by responsibility.

✔ Small interfaces improve flexibility and maintainability.

✔ ISP naturally helps prevent LSP violations.

✔ ASP.NET Core applications commonly apply ISP by separating reading, writing, deleting, reporting, and notification capabilities into different interfaces.

